Send the last Schannel handshake token on the output descriptor.
When InitializeSecurityContext completed the handshake and still had a
token for the peer, tls_handshake wrote it to state->ifd, the descriptor
it reads from, while every other token goes to state->ofd. ssl_client
on Windows passes one socket for both, so nothing breaks today, but a
caller with separate descriptors would have sent the token to the wrong
place and the peer would never have finished the handshake. Use ofd.

---
 networking/tls.c |    2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/networking/tls.c
+++ b/networking/tls.c
@@ -3652,7 +3652,7 @@
 						: 0;
 
 				if (out_buffers[0].cbBuffer > 0) {
-					xwrite(state->ifd, out_buffers[0].pvBuffer, out_buffers[0].cbBuffer);
+					xwrite(state->ofd, out_buffers[0].pvBuffer, out_buffers[0].cbBuffer);
 					FreeContextBuffer(out_buffers[0].pvBuffer);
 				}
 
